On Mon, Nov 16, 2026 at 7:01 PM William Chuang <user49@example.test> wrote: > When the filesystem has been withdrawn, gfs2_glock_nq() returns -EIO > without queuing the holder, and gfs2_glock_nq_init() then calls > gfs2_holder_uninit(), which sets gh.gh_gl to NULL. > > gfs2_seek_data() and gfs2_seek_hole() call gfs2_glock_dq_uninit() > unconditionally, so in that case gfs2_glock_dq() dereferences a NULL > glock when it takes gl->gl_lockref.lock, causing a general protection > fault. > > Only release the holder when it was successfully acquired, matching the > pattern already used elsewhere in this file (such as gfs2_fiemap() and > gfs2_getattr()). > > Fixes: 3a27411cb4bc ("gfs2: Implement SEEK_HOLE / SEEK_DATA via iomap") > Reported-by: user50@example.test > Closes: https://syzkaller.appspot.com/bug?extid=5a5f492ccae698fd7434 > Cc: user25@example.test > Signed-off-by: William Chuang <user49@example.test>
Thanks.
Could you please replace this with the following fix and resend?
Andreas
--
gfs2_seek_data() and gfs2_seek_hole() call gfs2_glock_nq_init(), and independent of the outcome, they call gfs2_glock_dq_uninit(). When the gfs2_glock_nq() call in gfs2_glock_nq_init() fails, gfs2_glock_nq_init() returns with an error code and gh.gh_gl is set NULL. This causes a NULL pointer dereference in gfs2_glock_dq().
Fix that by only calling gfs2_glock_dq_uninit() when gfs2_glock_nq_init() succeeds.
Fixes: 3a27411cb4bc ("gfs2: Implement SEEK_HOLE / SEEK_DATA via iomap") Reported-by: user50@example.test Closes: https://syzkaller.appspot.com/bug?extid=5a5f492ccae698fd7434 Cc: user25@example.test Signed-off-by: William Chuang <user49@example.test>
diff --git a/fs/gfs2/inode.c b/fs/gfs2/inode.c index 2c2dc459c037..d4678e4832b3 100644 --- a/fs/gfs2/inode.c +++ b/fs/gfs2/inode.c @@ -2253,9 +2253,11 @@ loff_t gfs2_seek_data(struct file *file, loff_t offset)
inode_lock_shared(inode); ret = gfs2_glock_nq_init(gl, LM_ST_SHARED, 0, &gh); - if (!ret) - ret = iomap_seek_data(inode, offset, &gfs2_iomap_ops); + if (ret) + goto out; + ret = iomap_seek_data(inode, offset, &gfs2_iomap_ops); gfs2_glock_dq_uninit(&gh); +out: inode_unlock_shared(inode);
if (ret < 0) @@ -2272,9 +2274,11 @@ loff_t gfs2_seek_hole(struct file *file, loff_t offset)
inode_lock_shared(inode); ret = gfs2_glock_nq_init(gl, LM_ST_SHARED, 0, &gh); - if (!ret) - ret = iomap_seek_hole(inode, offset, &gfs2_iomap_ops); + if (ret) + goto out; + ret = iomap_seek_hole(inode, offset, &gfs2_iomap_ops); gfs2_glock_dq_uninit(&gh); +out: inode_unlock_shared(inode);
if (ret < 0)

ecryptfs_encrypt_page(), ecryptfs_decrypt_page(), ecryptfs_read_lower_page_segment(), ecryptfs_write_lower_page_segment(), lower_offset_for_page() and fill_zeros_to_end_of_page() all take a folio now. Rename them accordingly.
Signed-off-by: Tal Zussman <user58@example.test> --- fs/ecryptfs/crypto.c | 16 ++++++++-------- fs/ecryptfs/ecryptfs_kernel.h | 8 ++++---- fs/ecryptfs/inode.c | 2 +- fs/ecryptfs/mmap.c | 26 +++++++++++++------------- fs/ecryptfs/read_write.c | 12 ++++++------ 5 files changed, 32 insertions(+), 32 deletions(-)
diff --git a/fs/ecryptfs/crypto.c b/fs/ecryptfs/crypto.c index f0e67b48917f..bbaeb9a38db0 100644 --- a/fs/ecryptfs/crypto.c +++ b/fs/ecryptfs/crypto.c @@ -212,11 +212,11 @@ static int crypt_scatterlist(struct ecryptfs_crypt_stat *crypt_stat, }
/* - * lower_offset_for_page + * lower_offset_for_folio * * Convert an eCryptfs page index into a lower byte offset */ -static loff_t lower_offset_for_page(struct ecryptfs_crypt_stat *crypt_stat, +static loff_t lower_offset_for_folio(struct ecryptfs_crypt_stat *crypt_stat, struct folio *folio) { return ecryptfs_lower_header_size(crypt_stat) + @@ -274,7 +274,7 @@ static int crypt_extent(struct ecryptfs_crypt_stat *crypt_stat, }
/** - * ecryptfs_encrypt_page + * ecryptfs_encrypt_folio * @folio: Folio mapped from the eCryptfs inode for the file; contains * decrypted content that needs to be encrypted (to a temporary * buffer; not in place) and written out to the lower file @@ -289,7 +289,7 @@ static int crypt_extent(struct ecryptfs_crypt_stat *crypt_stat, * * Returns zero on success; negative on error */ -int ecryptfs_encrypt_page(struct folio *folio) +int ecryptfs_encrypt_folio(struct folio *folio) { struct inode *ecryptfs_inode; struct ecryptfs_crypt_stat *crypt_stat; @@ -322,7 +322,7 @@ int ecryptfs_encrypt_page(struct folio *folio) } }
- lower_offset = lower_offset_for_page(crypt_stat, folio); + lower_offset = lower_offset_for_folio(crypt_stat, folio); rc = ecryptfs_write_lower(ecryptfs_inode, enc_extent_virt, lower_offset, PAGE_SIZE); if (rc < 0) { @@ -338,7 +338,7 @@ int ecryptfs_encrypt_page(struct folio *folio) }
/** - * ecryptfs_decrypt_page + * ecryptfs_decrypt_folio * @folio: Folio mapped from the eCryptfs inode for the file; data read * and decrypted from the lower file will be written into this * page @@ -353,7 +353,7 @@ int ecryptfs_encrypt_page(struct folio *folio) * * Returns zero on success; negative on error */ -int ecryptfs_decrypt_page(struct folio *folio) +int ecryptfs_decrypt_folio(struct folio *folio) { struct inode *ecryptfs_inode; struct ecryptfs_crypt_stat *crypt_stat; @@ -367,7 +367,7 @@ int ecryptfs_decrypt_page(struct folio *folio)

A shrinking truncate zeroes the rest of the folio that holds the new
end of the file by copying from the zero page through ecryptfs_write().
Zero the folio in place with folio_zero_range() instead, the way the
truncate helpers in other filesystems do, and encrypt it to the lower
file. This no longer bumps i_size to the end of the folio and writes
that to the metadata before the real size is written. It also removes
the last struct page usage in ecryptfs.

The folio stays locked while it is encrypted, as in
ecryptfs_writepages(), and is marked not uptodate if that fails, so a
later read goes back to the lower file instead of returning zeroes that
were never written out.

A page-aligned new size has no tail to zero, so skip it. The old code
wrote out a full page of zeroes past the new end of the file in that
case, which the truncate then discarded.

Signed-off-by: Tal Zussman <user58@example.test>
---
 fs/ecryptfs/inode.c | 55 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-------------
 1 file changed, 42 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/fs/ecryptfs/inode.c b/fs/ecryptfs/inode.c
index 525297c7ebd8..ffab685688ab 100644
--- a/fs/ecryptfs/inode.c
+++ b/fs/ecryptfs/inode.c
@@ -718,6 +718,40 @@ upper_size_to_lower_size(struct ecryptfs_crypt_stat *crypt_stat,
 	return lower_size;
 }

+/**
+ * ecryptfs_zero_tail
+ * @inode: The ecryptfs inode
+ * @pos: The file offset from which to zero
+ *
+ * Zero the folio that holds @pos from @pos to its end, and write the
+ * result out to the lower file.
+ *
+ * Returns zero on success; non-zero otherwise
+ */
+static int ecryptfs_zero_tail(struct inode *inode, loff_t pos)
+{
+	struct folio *folio;
+	size_t from;
+	int rc;
+
+	/* A page-aligned @pos has no tail to zero. */
+	if (!offset_in_page(pos))
+		return 0;
+
+	folio = read_mapping_folio(inode->i_mapping, pos >> PAGE_SHIFT, NULL);
+	if (IS_ERR(folio))
+		return PTR_ERR(folio);
+	from = offset_in_folio(folio, pos);
+	folio_lock(folio);
+	folio_zero_range(folio, from, folio_size(folio) - from);
+	rc = ecryptfs_encrypt_page(folio);
+	if (rc)
+		folio_clear_uptodate(folio);
+	folio_unlock(folio);
+	folio_put(folio);
+	return rc;
+}
+
 /**
  * __ecryptfs_truncate
  * @dentry: The ecryptfs layer dentry
@@ -739,7 +773,6 @@ static int __ecryptfs_truncate(struct dentry *dentry, const struct iattr *ia)
 	loff_t lower_size_before_truncate;
 	loff_t lower_size_after_truncate;
 	struct iattr lower_ia;
-	size_t num_zeros;
 	int rc;

 	ecryptfs_iattr_to_lower(&lower_ia, ia);
@@ -788,19 +821,15 @@ static int __ecryptfs_truncate(struct dentry *dentry, const struct iattr *ia)
 	}

 	/*
-	 * We're chopping off all the pages down to the page in which
-	 * ia->ia_size is located. Fill in the end of that page from
-	 * (ia->ia_size & ~PAGE_MASK) to PAGE_SIZE with zeros.
+	 * We're chopping off all the folios down to the folio in which
+	 * ia->ia_size is located. Fill in the end of that folio from
+	 * ia->ia_size with zeros.
 	 */
-	num_zeros = PAGE_SIZE - (ia->ia_size & ~PAGE_MASK);
-	if (num_zeros) {
-		rc = ecryptfs_write(inode, page_address(ZERO_PAGE(0)),

Every caller passes a kmalloc()-ed buffer or an array inside a kmalloc()-ed
structure, which is physically contiguous and so fits in one
scatterlist entry. Use sg_init_one(), which can't fail, drop the
two-entry arrays and their length checks, and remove
virt_to_scatterlist() and the unused ECRYPTFS_MAX_SCATTERLIST_LEN.

Signed-off-by: Tal Zussman <user58@example.test>
---
 fs/ecryptfs/crypto.c          |  46 -----------------
 fs/ecryptfs/ecryptfs_kernel.h |   2 -
 fs/ecryptfs/keystore.c        | 114 +++++++++---------------------------------
 3 files changed, 25 insertions(+), 137 deletions(-)

diff --git a/fs/ecryptfs/crypto.c b/fs/ecryptfs/crypto.c
index 6a014872e919..f0e67b48917f 100644
--- a/fs/ecryptfs/crypto.c
+++ b/fs/ecryptfs/crypto.c
@@ -149,50 +149,6 @@ void ecryptfs_destroy_mount_crypt_stat(
 	memset(mount_crypt_stat, 0, sizeof(struct ecryptfs_mount_crypt_stat));
 }

-/**
- * virt_to_scatterlist
- * @addr: Virtual address
- * @size: Size of data; should be an even multiple of the block size
- * @sg: Pointer to scatterlist array; set to NULL to obtain only
- *      the number of scatterlist structs required in array
- * @sg_size: Max array size
- *
- * Fills in a scatterlist array with page references for a passed
- * virtual address.
- *
- * Returns the number of scatterlist structs in array used
- */
-int virt_to_scatterlist(const void *addr, int size, struct scatterlist *sg,
-			int sg_size)
-{
-	int i = 0;
-	struct page *pg;
-	int offset;
-	int remainder_of_page;
-
-	sg_init_table(sg, sg_size);
-
-	while (size > 0 && i < sg_size) {
-		pg = virt_to_page(addr);
-		offset = offset_in_page(addr);
-		sg_set_page(&sg[i], pg, 0, offset);
-		remainder_of_page = PAGE_SIZE - offset;
-		if (size >= remainder_of_page) {
-			sg[i].length = remainder_of_page;
-			addr += remainder_of_page;
-			size -= remainder_of_page;
-		} else {
-			sg[i].length = size;
-			addr += size;
-			size = 0;
-		}
-		i++;
-	}
-	if (size > 0)
-		return -ENOMEM;
-	return i;
-}
-
 /**
  * crypt_scatterlist
  * @crypt_stat: Pointer to the crypt_stat struct to initialize.
@@ -438,8 +394,6 @@ int ecryptfs_decrypt_page(struct folio *folio)
 	return rc;
 }

-#define ECRYPTFS_MAX_SCATTERLIST_LEN 4
-
 /**
  * ecryptfs_init_crypt_ctx
  * @crypt_stat: Uninitialized crypt stats structure
diff --git a/fs/ecryptfs/ecryptfs_kernel.h b/fs/ecryptfs/ecryptfs_kernel.h
index d828040ade89..301744f0f819 100644
--- a/fs/ecryptfs/ecryptfs_kernel.h
+++ b/fs/ecryptfs/ecryptfs_kernel.h
@@ -544,8 +544,6 @@ int ecryptfs_encrypt_and_encode_filename(
 	struct ecryptfs_mount_crypt_stat *mount_crypt_stat,
 	const char *name, size_t name_size);
 void ecryptfs_dump_hex(char *data, int bytes);
-int virt_to_scatterlist(const void *addr, int size, struct scatterlist *sg,
-			int sg_size);
 int ecryptfs_compute_root_iv(struct ecryptfs_crypt_stat *crypt_stat);
 void ecryptfs_rotate_iv(unsigned char *iv);
 void ecryptfs_init_crypt_stat(struct ecryptfs_crypt_stat *crypt_stat);
diff --git a/fs/ecryptfs/keystore.c b/fs/ecryptfs/keystore.c
index a0989e3c8aef..b147ddb0055d 100644
--- a/fs/ecryptfs/keystore.c
+++ b/fs/ecryptfs/keystore.c

crypt_extent() currently takes its source and destination as pages, so ecryptfs_encrypt_page() and ecryptfs_decrypt_page() have to pass folio_page() of the folio they are given, and the ciphertext buffer in ecryptfs_encrypt_page() is allocated as a page to match.
The source is a page cache folio, so pass it as one and set up its scatterlist with sg_set_folio(). The destination is not: it is a temporary buffer that is only needed to be able to write to the lower file. There is no reason for it to be a page or folio. Convert it to a buffer instead, with NULL meaning the operation is done in place on the folio, which is what ecryptfs_decrypt_page() does. ecryptfs_encrypt_page() can then kmalloc() it and write it out without mapping it. Allocate the buffer with GFP_KERNEL. There was no reason to use GFP_USER as it had previously been doing.
Signed-off-by: Tal Zussman <user58@example.test> --- fs/ecryptfs/crypto.c | 47 ++++++++++++++++++++--------------------------- 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 27 deletions(-)
diff --git a/fs/ecryptfs/crypto.c b/fs/ecryptfs/crypto.c index e67119b6029c..9b8b0c5a81cd 100644 --- a/fs/ecryptfs/crypto.c +++ b/fs/ecryptfs/crypto.c @@ -271,8 +271,9 @@ static loff_t lower_offset_for_page(struct ecryptfs_crypt_stat *crypt_stat, * crypt_extent * @crypt_stat: crypt_stat containing cryptographic context for the * encryption operation - * @dst_page: The page to write the result into - * @src_page: The page to read from + * @folio: The folio to read from + * @dst: The buffer to write the result into, or NULL to crypt the + * folio in place * @page_index: The offset in the file (in units of PAGE_SIZE) * @extent_offset: Page extent offset for use in generating IV * @op: ENCRYPT or DECRYPT to indicate the desired operation @@ -282,14 +283,12 @@ static loff_t lower_offset_for_page(struct ecryptfs_crypt_stat *crypt_stat, * Return zero on success; non-zero otherwise */ static int crypt_extent(struct ecryptfs_crypt_stat *crypt_stat, - struct page *dst_page, - struct page *src_page, - pgoff_t page_index, + struct folio *folio, void *dst, pgoff_t page_index, unsigned long extent_offset, int op) { loff_t extent_base; char extent_iv[ECRYPTFS_MAX_IV_BYTES]; - struct scatterlist src_sg, dst_sg; + struct scatterlist src_sg, dst_sg, *dst_sgp = &src_sg; size_t extent_size = crypt_stat->extent_size; int rc;
@@ -297,14 +296,15 @@ static int crypt_extent(struct ecryptfs_crypt_stat *crypt_stat, ecryptfs_derive_iv(extent_iv, crypt_stat, extent_base + extent_offset);
sg_init_table(&src_sg, 1); - sg_init_table(&dst_sg, 1); - - sg_set_page(&src_sg, src_page, extent_size, - extent_offset * extent_size); - sg_set_page(&dst_sg, dst_page, extent_size, - extent_offset * extent_size); + sg_set_folio(&src_sg, folio, extent_size, + extent_offset * extent_size); + if (dst) { + sg_init_one(&dst_sg, dst + extent_offset * extent_size, + extent_size); + dst_sgp = &dst_sg; + }
